[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue, "dates parse mm/dd in eu locales". Counted by
cause instead of by subject line, it holds three different problems. Each needs its own ticket and
its own fix.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued fix covers the first problem only. The other two stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The three problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

1. **`parser`.** The shared parser reads every date month first. A day-first tenant's dates come out
   flipped. This is the bug #4471 was filed for, and it covers most of the reports.
2. **`hint`.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants
   type what the hint tells them, and the form reads it their way. The parser fix does not touch this.
3. **`one-day shift`.** The date moves by exactly one day, not by a month swap. One report names
   Lisbon and Toronto, and another says an invite arrived before the booking existed, so a time zone
   conversion is the lead. The cause is not confirmed.
   The desk's own notes (`desk-notes.json`) already read these reports as a one-day move, yet they were
   filed under the parser because of their tenant.

## How the count was made

- One report id counts once. The ticket system exports in overlapping pages, so the same report can
  come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant (its locale and booking form), as in `causes.ts`. Intake's tag is a
  guess made from the reporter's browser, and the last column shows how often it disagrees.
- The numbers are computed on every build. Nobody types them into this page.

## What to do

- Open one ticket per problem, each with the number of reports it covers.
- Do not close the `hint` and `one-day shift` reports when the parser fix ships.
- Replace [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) with a record that merges on cause.
  Merging on the subject line is how three problems became one ticket.

